{quote}I can answer only "up to you" :slightly_smiling_face:{quote}

I mean, we want to fix the issue, but we to know how. The chain looks like so: support automations -> commerce -> payments.
Here the payments are failing the commerce requests, and we need to know the reasons, so we can fix it.

Payments is supposed to add such validation on their side, as stated [here|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/CNV6QPRFT/p1764666522604149?thread_ts=1764605451.684799&cid=CNV6QPRFT].

I asked [@abhilekh.sahay|https://taxify.atlassian.net/jira/people/712020:76180c2c-9e2c-4717-8560-56ba59fe9cd8] how it was implemented and got reply to use this [method|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-nodejs-common/blob/develop/src/helpers/Monetary.ts#L923]: {{roundToCurrencyMinorUnits}}

But after implementing it it does not work as it is expected.
Example:

{noformat}export function createPartialCompensation(amount: number, currency: string): PartialCompensation {
    return {
        type: CompensationType.PARTIAL_REFUND,
        amount: Monetary.of(amount, currency).roundToCurrencyMinorUnits().amount.toNumber(),
    };
}{noformat}

Unit test:

{noformat}        it("should create partial compensation with currency KES", () => {
            // Arrange
            const amount = 147.1412;
            const currency = "KES";

            // Act
            const result: PartialCompensation = createPartialCompensation(amount, currency);

            // Assert
            expect(result).toEqual({
                type: CompensationType.PARTIAL_REFUND,
                amount: 147,
            });
        });{noformat}

Output:

{noformat}    expect(received).toEqual(expected) // deep equality

    - Expected  - 1
    + Received  + 1

      Object {
    -   "amount": 147,
    +   "amount": 147.14,
        "type": "partial_refund",
      }

      77 |
      78 |             // Assert
    > 79 |             expect(result).toEqual({
         |                            ^
      80 |                 type: CompensationType.PARTIAL_REFUND,
      81 |                 amount: 147,
      82 |             });{noformat}

That method still rounds to 2 decimals which matches the config [@przemyslaw.wiczolek|https://taxify.atlassian.net/jira/people/712020:f3b3e918-2599-4c21-991c-efa4dcddf08d] shared above.

In {{balance_user}}  we can find in code this [method|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/7cc2d6a6887b00e27e6b5ca315e9ab7b70b1bda4/src/platform/commerce/balance_user/main/service/BookingService.ts#L1104]:

{noformat}    public async isAmountAsPerMinimalUnitConfig(amount: tMonetary) {
        const tAmount = Monetary(amount);
        const config = this.config.minimal_unit_adjustment_config.value.currency_config.find((c) =>
            tAmount.isSameCurrency(c.currency)
        );
        if (config && config.enabled) {
            //TODO: add minimal unit check from NJSC once the PR with new methods is merged
            if (!tAmount.roundToUnit(config.minimal_unit).eq(amount)) {
                throw BoltError.errorWithText(
                    CommonResponseCodes.INVALID_REQUEST,
                    `Amount ${tAmount} is not fits to minimal unit`
                );
            }
        }
    }{noformat}

This method rounds with {{roundToUnit}} every decimal number to round number (for example: 147.1412 -> 147)

Which is using admin panel [config|https://admin-panel.bolt.eu/dynamic-config/entity/edit/YmFsYW5jZS11c2VyL21pbmltYWxfdW5pdF9hZGp1c3RtZW50X2NvbmZpZw==/:shard]:

{noformat}{
    "currency_config": [
        {
            "currency": "kes",
            "enabled": true,
            "minimal_unit": 1
        }
    ]
}{noformat}

Please assist [@michal.buzek|https://taxify.atlassian.net/jira/people/637dde52fde064eda2edd04b] on solving this mess. I would like to avoid any custom implementations for amount rounding implemented in our Support Automations. We want to know what is expected on commerce and payments side when it comes to refunds in KES currency.

In our code we already use {{roundToCurrencyMinorUnits}} helper for setting the amounts.

